## Concrete next dependency and limits

The next bounded dependency is **isolated versioned proof registration plus its v2 eligibility predicate and exact inactive transport**, with actual SQL composition tests. Current `bfa_p5_voice_register_proof` accepts an unversioned payload, can adopt unbound cleanup, and calls the v1 `eligible()` helper. That helper and `bfa_p5_capacity.current/take_proof` still require an expiring legacy session fence and lack protocol checks. A v2 issuer/cleanup record cannot safely be routed through them.

Implement a separate v2 proof registrar outside migrations, requiring explicit protocol 2, the exact already-bound cleanup/claim/parent/fingerprint and current trusted identity/policy. Reuse the existing proof table with a reviewed nullable protocol discriminator (legacy NULL, new 2), preserving original rowtype-based legacy insertion; no duplicate accounting table. Validate current parent/version, every legacy-fence exclusion, associated immutable issuance, current thread/generation/allowlist and exact approval at the proof stage. Preserve one receipt per operation, conservative <30000-ms freshness, immutable expiry bounds, shrink-only cleanup/claim ceilings and terminal precedence. Proof storage is not consumption, admission or provider permission. Test no legacy/unbound proof/cleanup adoption, roles, late/lost replies, current-state changes, legacy rowtype compatibility and unchanged cumulative holds. No v1 predicate fallback.

New v2 proof registration and later consumption/admission/capacity remain separately unfinished. In particular, the old callable proof path can still operate on shared rows if a legacy fence is later introduced; this discriminator alone does not establish compatibility. The entire chain remains inactive/nondeployable until versioned consumers and the quiesced no-fallback route/privilege cutover are complete. Native concurrent/back-end races, deployed Auth ownership/policy/revocation and provider/device finality remain unrun. Existing exact close/reconcile paths must remain available after expiry/revocation with their original accounting semantics.

